{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged","id":183891,"title":"Supreme Court Term Review [merged]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-06-29T11:14:41-07:00","id":183891,"title":"Supreme Court Term Review [merged]","content":"Since there's not a general politics thread (I don't think)... here's a good one for a Supreme Court review for 2013-2014. Has a nice roll-up of the court cases decided to date from constitutional law professors on all sides of the political spectrum. <a href=\"http://www.c-span.org/video/?320194-1/supreme-court-term-review\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court Review video.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T06:19:10-07:00","id":184748,"title":"","content":"A lot of pieces to a puzzle that are making one screwed up picture to me...The Supreme Court's decisions are just more pieces.<br /> The police terrorizing the wrong people seem to be more of thing these days.<br /> And then there's spying on American citizens...and the coordination of the <a href=\"http://www.motherjones.com/politics/2013/06/nsa-spying-occupy-homeland-security\" target=\"_blank\">crackdown on legitimate protest groups</a> in which the FBI assisted.<br /> And the gods' forbid you say out anything about some of the shenanigans that the government is doing. A record number of <a href=\"http://www.motherjones.com/politics/2012/06/obamas-whistleblowers-stuxnet-leaks-drones\" target=\"_blank\">whistleblowers have been prosecuted for leaking the wrong things</a>...(If you leak something that benefits the admin then it's ok)<br /> Although it's good to hear the wealthy are finally getting nervous; <a href=\"http://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/06/the-pitchforks-are-coming-for-us-plutocrats-108014.html\" target=\"_blank\">The Pitchforks are Coming for Us Plutocrats</a><br /> I'm not seeing any of this stuff reversing itself anytime soon. And since that's the case, where is this going to eventually lead? Add in the fact that the <a href=\"http://economy.money.cnn.com/2014/01/28/middle-class/\" target=\"_blank\">Middle Class is Disappearing</a> It's just not painting a pretty picture. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","user":"Shadowflame","date":". 06-30-2014, 09:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T08:54:26-07:00","id":184857,"title":"","content":"Welp the US supreme court has ruled that Hobby Lobby is right and just in saying that because the company is of a particular religion, they can forbid birth control to be covered by their employee healthcare without issue or penalty.<br /> <br /> I personally disagree with it on multiple levels.<br /> <br /> The biggest of which is that it allows corporations to define a religion or set of beliefs and enforce them upon employees.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T09:23:16-07:00","id":184888,"title":"","content":"Yeah, read that here; <a href=\"http://thehill.com/regulation/healthcare/210948-supreme-court-rejects-obama-limits-birth-control-mandate\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court rejects Obama, limits birth control mandate</a><br /> <i><br /> The Supreme Court delivered a blow to President Obama\u2019s signature healthcare law on Monday, ruling that closely held companies cannot be compelled to offer their employees birth control as part of the law if they object to the provisions on religious grounds.<br /> The 5-4 ruling written by conservative Justice Samuel Alito undermines the Affordable Care Act\u2019s requirement that companies offer contraceptive services to workers as part of their insurance coverage, potentially affecting millions of women.<br /> But it leaves the requirement intact for publicly traded companies and, in a concurring opinion, Justice Anthony Kennedy argues that the government could decide to fill the void by covering the costs of the coverage itself.</i><br /> -----------------------<br /> I was more concerned over the Union ruling which still seems iffy; <a href=\"http://thehill.com/regulation/court-battles/210944-unions-dodge-bullet-at-supreme-court\" target=\"_blank\">Unions dodge bullet at Supreme Court</a><br /> <i><br /> In a 5-4 decision, the justices voided a requirement that personal medical assistants in Illinois contribute fees to a branch of Service Employees International Union (SEIU), even if they choose not to become members.<br /> But in a victory for labor, the justices did not directly address the broader question of whether public sector employees can be forced to pay union dues, leaving in place state laws aimed at preventing \u201cfree riding\u201d by non-members.</i><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T09:48:21-07:00","id":184917,"title":"","content":"With the union thing I can at least see both sides of the issue as having rational validity. Yes, employers can, do and will &quot;strongly encourage&quot; employees to not join a union which will be to their advantage.<br /> <br /> On the flip side, I've seen unions that were little more than leeches on the workers because they had grown too large and secure. They had become little more than a second set of managers, taking advantage of the fact that all people in the organization had no choice but sign up with them.<br /> <br /> My own personal tendency in the unionized worker situation would be to take the time to establish contracts that are made to auto-adjust over time, resulting in dedicated union employees (outside of basic administration) generally only being needed every few decades instead of ever couple of years.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T09:49:45-07:00","id":184918,"title":"","content":"God damn it, Supreme Court, <i>words mean things</i> and corporations are not people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T10:26:19-07:00","id":184938,"title":"","content":"Well, if I were a woman applying for a job that is tied up to a religious cause, I should think I'd know what to expect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T11:06:27-07:00","id":184956,"title":"","content":"The problem is that specifically religious groups, like churches, already have exemptions.<br /> <br /> Hobby Lobby isn't a religious cause. It's a commercial enterprise. They sell goods. The company doesn't offer any sort of religious services. The only thing that makes Hobby Lobby special is that it is privately owned instead of a publicly traded company. Which... isn't actually all that special because &quot;closely held&quot; companies are very common in the USA and employ a staggering number of people.<br /> <br /> Saying that these companies have religious identities tied to their owners is insanity.<br /> <br /> The stupidest thing is that, apparently, we can't force Hobby Lobby to provide proper health coverage, but the government can pay for the gaps in coverage... with taxes... paid for by people; including Hobby Lobby's owners.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T12:48:05-07:00","id":184999,"title":"","content":"You can't discriminate because of religion but your non-religion religion company can discriminate about an individuals non/other religions point of view on the subject of birth control. Makes perfect sense to me not in the least.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forever and always forward.<br /> Don't ask why that came to mind or that I felt the need to post it.<br /> I see the incorporeally challenged.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/787-sorwen","name":"Sorwen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T14:53:04-07:00","id":185078,"title":"","content":"Without agreeing or disagreeing with the Supreme Court: the Court said that closely held private corporations are entities formed by people to serve as an extension of their corporate will. Thus, they believe it follows that a closely held, private, corporation can hold a religious stance and is protected under 1993's law.<br /> <br /> This ruling does not apply to all corporations and it certainly doesn't apply to public corporations.<br /> <br /> In like manner, closely held private corporations have been able to successfully discriminate on the basis of religion, Sorwen.<br /> <br /> By the way: [url=http://www.c-span.org/video/?318462-1/sebelius-v-hobby-lobby-stores-oral-argument]here's the oral arguments.[/quote] Try giving it a listen. May not be as cut-and-dry on either side as you think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-30-2014, 05:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T14:54:50-07:00","id":185080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184748#post184748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of pieces to a puzzle that are making one screwed up picture to me...The Supreme Court's decisions are just more pieces.<br /> The police terrorizing the wrong people seem to be more of thing these days.<br /> And then there's spying on American citizens...and the coordination of the <a href=\"http://www.motherjones.com/politics/2013/06/nsa-spying-occupy-homeland-security\" target=\"_blank\">crackdown on legitimate protest groups</a> in which the FBI assisted.<br /> And the gods' forbid you say out anything about some of the shenanigans that the government is doing. A record number of <a href=\"http://www.motherjones.com/politics/2012/06/obamas-whistleblowers-stuxnet-leaks-drones\" target=\"_blank\">whistleblowers have been prosecuted for leaking the wrong things</a>...(If you leak something that benefits the admin then it's ok)<br /> Although it's good to hear the wealthy are finally getting nervous; <a href=\"http://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/06/the-pitchforks-are-coming-for-us-plutocrats-108014.html\" target=\"_blank\">The Pitchforks are Coming for Us Plutocrats</a><br /> I'm not seeing any of this stuff reversing itself anytime soon. And since that's the case, where is this going to eventually lead? Add in the fact that the <a href=\"http://economy.money.cnn.com/2014/01/28/middle-class/\" target=\"_blank\">Middle Class is Disappearing</a> It's just not painting a pretty picture. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>So which court cases backed each of those linked items?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T14:55:47-07:00","id":185081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184917#post184917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With the union thing I can at least see both sides of the issue as having rational validity. Yes, employers can, do and will &quot;strongly encourage&quot; employees to not join a union which will be to their advantage.<br /> <br /> On the flip side, I've seen unions that were little more than leeches on the workers because they had grown too large and secure. They had become little more than a second set of managers, taking advantage of the fact that all people in the organization had no choice but sign up with them.<br /> <br /> My own personal tendency in the unionized worker situation would be to take the time to establish contracts that are made to auto-adjust over time, resulting in dedicated union employees (outside of basic administration) generally only being needed every few decades instead of ever couple of years.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This ruling has actually been about 30 years in the making... since Carter broke the Federal unions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T15:02:21-07:00","id":185090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184918#post184918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">God damn it, Supreme Court, <i>words mean things</i> and corporations are not people.</div> </div> </div> </div>...well it's a little muddier than that. The free exercise clause protects an activity, not people. The Right to peaceably assemble and petition for redress is what's guaranteed to the people. In Federalist 10, Madison actually talked about how factions would rise up and incorporate and that this freedom should be understood to be theirs as well, and let the people decide what is true or false.<br /> <br /> Saying this is something new is patently false, but our understanding of it has morphed and changed, back and forth, over time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T15:04:51-07:00","id":185105,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184956#post184956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Saying that these companies have religious identities tied to their owners is insanity.</div> </div> </div> </div> Why?<br /> <br /> Before anyone gets (more) upset, keep in mind I'm not taking a stance... I'm probing and desiring thought and conversation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T16:09:21-07:00","id":185187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185105#post185105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Why?<br /> <br /> Before anyone gets (more) upset, keep in mind I'm not taking a stance... I'm probing and desiring thought and conversation.</div> </div> </div> </div>In the abstract? Religion and religious entities are granted additional benefits beyond that which the secular world are afforded.<br /> <br /> This has classically been offset by limitations on both the individuals granted the greatest additional rights as well as the organizations they form... churches may be tax except but they're also expected to have social value and community support, and rapidly fail without them.<br /> <br /> That limitation is now removed, as for-profit organizations can now directly claim a religious position. Companies now are free to operate employee relations as if those employees hold the same faith, regardless of if they do or not. Besides the issue of granting additional powers to non secular organizations, besides the issue of corporations being granted more power over employees as an abstract thing, this has the double effect of allowing companies to enforce religious practice (if not belief) upon employees, as well as setting up a very messy situations with trust funds going forward.<br /> <br /> The first sets up the situation for general abuses of employees in the name of faith. if the founder of a company devoutly believes that medicine is a lie and the only valid treatment is homeopathy, they are welcome to deny all real medical care. This can also spill further into proscribing activities out-of-work that go against the belief structure of the company... and this can easily expand from Religion to Belief Structure. Work at a store founded by vegans and happen to eat meat? Not anymore, or else. Further it establishes a very generic grounds for dismissal. This is a Christian company, a worker accidentally stayed to 12:05am one early Sunday morning and failed to stone themselves for their crime, dismissed with cause. Or they ate shrimp, or failed to accept Jesus as their lord and savior for 5 minutes or anything else. A wide range of abuses.<br /> <br /> The second is that with the ruling you can now have Trust Funds with religious ties. Meaning we can have companies that are operating and enforcing a particular set of religious views even long after the direct owners have died.<br /> <br /> <br /> And of course in the modern quest to establish tax havens and control, it would be rather simple to set up a shell religious organization to take all the profit from something pay tax on it, turn around and reinvest it into the same companies that formed the religious organization. You end up with the church having extensive ownership of the company, but since that's just a control feedback loop there's no real problem for the company.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T16:27:36-07:00","id":185193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185187#post185187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That limitation is now removed, as for-profit organizations can now directly claim a religious position. Companies now are free to operate employee relations as if those employees hold the same faith, regardless of if they do or not. Besides the issue of granting additional powers to non secular organizations, besides the issue of corporations being granted more power over employees as an abstract thing, this has the double effect of allowing companies to enforce religious practice (if not belief) upon employees, as well as setting up a very messy situations with trust funds going forward.</div> </div> </div> </div>Only certain ones. It is actually more narrowly defined than corporations. Closely held private corporations is a special subset. Now, granting that RFRA has not been used in this way before, I want to make sure we're talking apples-to-apples here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The first sets up the situation for general abuses of employees in the name of faith. if the founder of a company devoutly believes that medicine is a lie and the only valid treatment is homeopathy, they are welcome to deny all real medical care. </div> </div> </div>In fact, the ruling explicitly said this is not the case, and that such trials would have to be separate and likely to fail (Justice Alito's words, citing vaccines, blood transfusions, etc).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This can also spill further into proscribing activities out-of-work that go against the belief structure of the company... and this can easily expand from Religion to Belief Structure. Work at a store founded by vegans and happen to eat meat? Not anymore, or else. </div> </div> </div> No, again... the exact wording applies it to recognized religions, not general belief structures, which have yet to be formally recognized as a thing by the Federal government.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Further it establishes a very generic grounds for dismissal. This is a Christian company, a worker accidentally stayed to 12:05am one early Sunday morning and failed to stone themselves for their crime, dismissed with cause. Or they ate shrimp, or failed to accept Jesus as their lord and savior for 5 minutes or anything else. A wide range of abuses. </div> </div> </div> Maybe. Again, the ruling was quite narrow and prior RFRA cases have already upheld that you can do no such thing that would be contrary to established law, where said law would not create undue burden. For example, stone yourself or be fired would be covered under various State laws, and has been upheld in the Supreme Court even since RFRA was passed. In at-will states, you can be fired for almost any reason... so it's easy enough to pull this off already in the states where this is likely to come up (as at-will states tend to be more right-leaning anyway).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The second is that with the ruling you can now have Trust Funds with religious ties. Meaning we can have companies that are operating and enforcing a particular set of religious views even long after the direct owners have died. </div> </div> </div> ...actually Trust Funds have a different tax status and filing, so it remains unknown whether or not this would still hold sway or if, because of the lack of person directing the trust fund, the same exemption would apply.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And of course in the modern quest to establish tax havens and control, it would be rather simple to set up a shell religious organization to take all the profit from something pay tax on it, turn around and reinvest it into the same companies that formed the religious organization. You end up with the church having extensive ownership of the company, but since that's just a control feedback loop there's no real problem for the company. </div> </div> </div>That would run afoul of IRS code and articles of incorporation currently in the Federal Code for corporation. Could it be done? I honestly don't know... and I'd recommend hiring a really, really, really smart lawyer before trying it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T16:38:46-07:00","id":185197,"title":"","content":"*shrugs* Only the religious conservative agreed on the Hobby Lobby thing. Hmm...curious; <a href=\"http://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/211014-some-religious-groups-oppose-hobby-lobby-decision\" target=\"_blank\">Some religious groups oppose Hobby Lobby decision</a>; <i> \u201cIf you are pro-religious liberty and pro-life and family, you can't support allowing a for-profit corporation to use religion to deny contraceptive coverage,\u201d says Rev. Richard Cizik, former vice president of the National Association of Evangelicals. The United Church of Christ, which says it represents more than a million Protestants, echoed the same sentiments following the ruling and said it sets bad precedence for what health coverage employers may decide to deny. \u201cWe believe that individuals, not for-profit corporations, should be guaranteed true religious liberty \u2014 the right to make decisions by following their own moral conscience and religious beliefs,\u201d said Sandy Soreson, the church\u2019s Washington, D.C., director.</i> ...Interesting take, and one I certainly support.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T17:07:54-07:00","id":185216,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185078#post185078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the Court said that closely held private corporations are entities formed by people to serve as an extension of their corporate will. Thus, they believe it follows that a closely held, private, corporation can hold a religious stance and is protected under 1993's law.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok... on what legal grounds?<br /> <br /> The Supreme Court doesn't exist to create law, it exists to rule on the validity of laws. The Supreme Court doesn't get to define what a corporation is. Corporations aren't constitutional entities. They only have whatever rights the legislature grants them.<br /> <br /> Corporations are also not the only form of legally recognized organization. People that want to exercise their religious freedoms in an organized fashion have plenty of was to do this that don't require corporations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This ruling does not apply to all corporations and it certainly doesn't apply to public corporations. </div> </div> </div>Closely held corporations can be publicly traded. The majority control is just in the hands of a very small group of people to the point that any shares available to the public are meaningless.<br /> <br /> And considering it applies to ~50% of the employees in the USA... this is not a limited issue.<br /> <br /> I haven't seen any reliable statistics on this, but that something that could impact half the workforce in the USA means it will have a huge impact by also impacting dependents.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> May not be as cut-and-dry on either side as you think. </div> </div> </div>The &quot;cut and dry&quot; might not be so clear from a legal standpoint. However I've yet to see any argument that the power this grants to corporations isn't cut and dry clear, and bad.<br /> <br /> Which isn't a legal argument, but since the Court effectively was ruling on two opposing interests more than anything else, they could have put the interests of the employees into a bit more focus than the majority did.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185090#post185090\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In Federalist 10, Madison actually talked about how factions would rise up and incorporate and that this freedom should be understood to be theirs as well, and let the people decide what is true or false.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure Madison's essays are not case law or precedent.<br /> <br /> What Madison thought doesn't matter to what the law should be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Saying this is something new is patently false, but our understanding of it has morphed and changed, back and forth, over time. </div> </div> </div>I think this is a pretty spurious point.<br /> <br /> While the stance on corporations might have waved a bit over time, the recent court decisions have put unprecedented power and rights into corporate hands despite the fact that the power of corporations was always something that was supposed to be controlled by the legislature.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185105#post185105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Why?</div> </div> </div> </div>Odd_Canuck covered most of it. But it is simple, the Court's ruling has put a corporation's owner's religious exercise of freedom to be more important than the personal exercise of religious freedom of their employees.<br /> <br /> As well, as I noted earlier, the Court has said that we can't force closely held corporations violate their owner's religious liberty by making covering birth-control mandatory. But we can violate their religious liberty by taxing them and pay for it that way instead.<br /> <br /> It is completely illogical. We can't say, &quot;you have to pay for this directly,&quot; but we can say, &quot;you have to pay us, so we'll pay for it instead.&quot; Insanity. There is, literally, nothing stopping Congress from putting in a &quot;birth control&quot; tax on corporations to pay for the stuff some corporations will refuse to cover.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Before anyone gets (more) upset, keep in mind I'm not taking a stance... I'm probing and desiring thought and conversation. </div> </div> </div>Just remember, while probing has its place, there are plenty of things that doesn't deserve devil's advocacy in polite discourse. This isn't a legal forum... if you know your probing is going to upset people... it is worth examining how important that probing is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T17:37:05-07:00","id":185239,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2014/06/30/a-lot-of-people-could-be-affected-by-the-supreme-courts-birth-control-decision/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/...trol-decision/</a> <i> So what exactly is a &quot;closely held corporation?&quot; And how many people will this effect? Some initially thought that this verbiage effectively narrowed the decision to a limited number of businesses, but it's actually the same language Hobby Lobby sought in seeking its exemption from the contraception mandate. It's also a term that covers a lot of businesses and Americans -- like, a lot. Basically, &quot;closely held&quot; is a term that covers as much as 90 percent (or more) of all businesses, according to a 2000 study. But while it covers the vast majority of employers, it doesn't necessarily cover the vast majority of employees. That's because publicly traded companies tend to have many more employees than private ones. Still, according to studies from Columbia University and New York University, closely held corporations employed 52 percent of the American workforce and accounted for slightly more than half -- 51 percent -- of economic output from the private sector. But does that mean the employers of half of all Americans will suddenly nix contraception coverage? Of course not, for several reasons: First, according to a Kaiser Family Foundation poll, 85 percent of large employers had already offered contraception coverage before Obamacare mandated it. And while Hobby Lobby fought that mandate, so far few other large companies have joined them. Second, federal law makes clear that companies seeking religious exemptions from such mandates must demonstrate sincere religious beliefs. In other words, it's not like they can just suddenly decide their religion precludes them from paying for birth control and stop doing so. And lastly, the vast majority of small businesses (96 percent, according to Kaiser) don't have to abide by the contraception mandate because they have fewer than 50 full-time employees. But, again, larger companies employ a hugely disproportionate percentage of the workforce -- which means a significant chunk of Americans work for large closely held corporations. Could other &quot;closely held corporations&quot; who previously complied with the mandate change their tune in the wake of today's ruling? Sure. And the numbers above suggest there's at least the theoretical possibility that more than half of employed Americans could lose their contraception coverage.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T17:38:13-07:00","id":185241,"title":"","content":"Sorry for the crappy format. This pc doesn't like this website...at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T18:25:28-07:00","id":185283,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185239#post185239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2014/06/30/a-lot-of-people-could-be-affected-by-the-supreme-courts-birth-control-decision/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/...trol-decision/</a> <i> So what exactly is a &quot;closely held corporation?&quot; And how many people will this effect? Some initially thought that this verbiage effectively narrowed the decision to a limited number of businesses, but it's actually the same language Hobby Lobby sought in seeking its exemption from the contraception mandate. It's also a term that covers a lot of businesses and Americans -- like, a lot. Basically, &quot;closely held&quot; is a term that covers as much as 90 percent (or more) of all businesses, according to a 2000 study. But while it covers the vast majority of employers, it doesn't necessarily cover the vast majority of employees. That's because publicly traded companies tend to have many more employees than private ones. Still, according to studies from Columbia University and New York University, closely held corporations employed 52 percent of the American workforce and accounted for slightly more than half -- 51 percent -- of economic output from the private sector. But does that mean the employers of half of all Americans will suddenly nix contraception coverage? Of course not, for several reasons: First, according to a Kaiser Family Foundation poll, 85 percent of large employers had already offered contraception coverage before Obamacare mandated it. And while Hobby Lobby fought that mandate, so far few other large companies have joined them. Second, federal law makes clear that companies seeking religious exemptions from such mandates must demonstrate sincere religious beliefs. In other words, it's not like they can just suddenly decide their religion precludes them from paying for birth control and stop doing so. And lastly, the vast majority of small businesses (96 percent, according to Kaiser) don't have to abide by the contraception mandate because they have fewer than 50 full-time employees. But, again, larger companies employ a hugely disproportionate percentage of the workforce -- which means a significant chunk of Americans work for large closely held corporations. Could other &quot;closely held corporations&quot; who previously complied with the mandate change their tune in the wake of today's ruling? Sure. And the numbers above suggest there's at least the theoretical possibility that more than half of employed Americans could lose their contraception coverage.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>That's a 2000 study... It's 14 years out of date. It also doesn't take into account 14 years of legislative changes and Dodd Frank.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T18:36:32-07:00","id":185288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185216#post185216\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ok... on what legal grounds?</div> </div> </div> </div> RFRA.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Supreme Court doesn't exist to create law, it exists to rule on the validity of laws. The Supreme Court doesn't get to define what a corporation is. Corporations aren't constitutional entities. They only have whatever rights the legislature grants them. </div> </div> </div> Marbury v. Madison and about 2000 years of Western Jurisprudence disagrees. Case law is entirely created by the courts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Corporations are also not the only form of legally recognized organization. People that want to exercise their religious freedoms in an organized fashion have plenty of was to do this that don't require corporations. </div> </div> </div> Like?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Closely held corporations can be publicly traded. The majority control is just in the hands of a very small group of people to the point that any shares available to the public are meaningless. </div> </div> </div> That's not the only limitation actually. IRS is clear (*snicker*) on that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I haven't seen any reliable statistics on this, but that something that could impact half the workforce in the USA means it will have a huge impact by also impacting dependents. </div> </div> </div> A lot of things could do that... a nuclear weapon, for instance.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The &quot;cut and dry&quot; might not be so clear from a legal standpoint. However I've yet to see any argument that the power this grants to corporations isn't cut and dry clear, and bad. </div> </div> </div> What power?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> [Which isn't a legal argument, but since the Court effectively was ruling on two opposing interests more than anything else, they could have put the interests of the employees into a bit more focus than the majority did. </div> </div> </div> How do you know they didn't? Did you read the ruling?<br /> <br /> Also, didn't you just say the Court is not the place of creating law? If that's so and they obeyed the law-as-written (which the majority will argue they did with RFRA and 1st Amendment), then wouldn't putting the interests of the employee above the minority, the employer, create law?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm pretty sure Madison's essays are not case law or precedent.<br /> <br /> What Madison thought doesn't matter to what the law should be. </div> </div> </div> lolwut?<br /> <br /> They are not only cited, quite often, they are perfectly valid ways to argue the Constitution in front of the Supreme Court and have been since... well... about 1799 or so (Calder v. Bull, Hines v. Davidowitz, McCullough v Maryland... in all, over 206 cases from 1799 to the mid 1980s, after that I'm not quite sure off the top of my head).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think this is a pretty spurious point.<br /> <br /> While the stance on corporations might have waved a bit over time, the recent court decisions have put unprecedented power and rights into corporate hands despite the fact that the power of corporations was always something that was supposed to be controlled by the legislature. </div> </div> </div> They have? Like? And yes, the power to incorporate is explicitly granted by the Constitution... as a measure and power of the Federal Government that exists to allow the Government (a corporation, albeit a very special one) to exist as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Odd_Canuck covered most of it. But it is simple, the Court's ruling has put a corporation's owner's religious exercise of freedom to be more important than the personal exercise of religious freedom of their employees.<br /> <br /> As well, as I noted earlier, the Court has said that we can't force closely held corporations violate their owner's religious liberty by making covering birth-control mandatory. But we can violate their religious liberty by taxing them and pay for it that way instead.<br /> <br /> It is completely illogical. We can't say, &quot;you have to pay for this directly,&quot; but we can say, &quot;you have to pay us, so we'll pay for it instead.&quot; Insanity. There is, literally, nothing stopping Congress from putting in a &quot;birth control&quot; tax on corporations to pay for the stuff some corporations will refuse to cover. </div> </div> </div> Couple of things: first, both legislature and the courts have given the reasons for that before, and did in this case too. Compelling interest of the state that shows burden is due for taxation (and that taxation can easily be a non-sincerely held belief to escape taxation - I believe one case in the 1990s actually had a Justice quote the book of Matthew in his decision: render unto Caesar).<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, this can be nothing but a sincerely held belief or else it would make little to no sense to do, given that you're paying less than $50 an employee (see the video I linked earlier, maybe 2 hours in or so... I think).<br /> <br /> Second, there is something stopping the government. The same ultimate check that exists to control the government on everything: the people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just remember, while probing has its place, there are plenty of things that doesn't deserve devil's advocacy in polite discourse. This isn't a legal forum... if you know your probing is going to upset people... it is worth examining how important that probing is. </div> </div> </div> Yet this forum could probably use a lot of education (except maybe the Canuck) on legal theory before they nerd rage... especially if they want to change things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-30-2014, 09:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:07:52-07:00","id":185293,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.slate.com/blogs/moneybox/2014/06/30/hobby_lobby_supreme_court_ruling_how_many_people_work_at_closely_held_corporations.html\" target=\"_blank\">How Many People Could the Hobby Lobby Ruling Affect?</a><br /> <i>The IRS defines a closely held corporation as one that, in general, has &quot;more than 50 percent of the value of its outstanding stock owned (directly or indirectly) by five or fewer individuals at any time during the last half of the tax year&quot; and &quot;is not a personal service corporation.&quot; These corporations are thought to make up around 90 percent of companies in the U.S. Many are small businesses, but large operations such as Koch Industries can also be closely held\u2014a fact Nancy Pelosi cited in her statement denouncing the decision.<br /> <br /> Hobby Lobby has 572 stores across the country. According to a 2009 research paper from NYU Stern School of Business, these corporations account for 52 percent of private employment and 51 percent of private-sector output in the country. Those percentages might be outdated now but still give a sense of just how many workers are employed at closely held corporations. <b>Fifty-two percent of today's private sector employees comes out to approximately 60.4 million people, based on the latest data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics</b>. </i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","user":"Shadowflame","date":".  spacing 07-01-2014, 08:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:10:17-07:00","id":185295,"title":"","content":"That's just lazy journalism. That quote comes right from the <a href=\"http://www.irs.gov/Help-&amp;-Resources/Tools-&amp;-FAQs/FAQs-for-Individuals/Frequently-Asked-Tax-Questions-&amp;-Answers/Small-Business,-Self-Employed,-Other-Business/Entities/Entities-5\" target=\"_blank\">IRS FAQ...</a> which states that and then directs you to the <a href=\"http://www.irs.gov/publications/p542/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">publication,</a> which then lists the other criteria for the &quot;in general&quot; to apply and a whole host of exceptions atop that.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, 90% of businesses in the US is a pretty misleading statistic, considering how many hundreds of thousands, if not millions, are self-employed, self-declared small businesses that are really just somebody incorporating to write off their lunches as a business expense (that's an exaggeration of why you do it, of course, but you can grossly summarize the reason as that). But it's the statistic everyone loves to throw around when it's in their favor, and when it's not they'll just ignore it and claim X doesn't affect that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-30-2014, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:12:21-07:00","id":185298,"title":"","content":"I don't think you favor the more liberal view here that this decision was shitty for women in general to begin with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:19:48-07:00","id":185300,"title":"","content":"Your inference does not equal my implication. Nor have I stated an opinion on whether or not I think the court was correct.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I will admit, however, that I am well and truly biased against Exalted 3rd Edition, but that's not terribly relevant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 06-30-2014, 10:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:29:05-07:00","id":185303,"title":"","content":"I do not recommend approaching a conversation with Nobody from a &quot;what he thinks of it&quot; angle. I haven't read every single post he's ever written, but I have not personally ever seen him express an opinion of his own, or confirm or deny guesses made about his opinions.<br /> <br /> So if we could move on from that line of conversation, that would be cool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:35:28-07:00","id":185308,"title":"","content":"And yet you defend such an opinion. And claim that you're more educated on this than those that oppose it. (except &quot;maybe&quot; the Canuck) But it's all about how well it's presented. And if it looks and smells like poo for most women, then I should think that it is what it is. I don't need a taste test here. Politics will be as usual..at play. And thanks to the Supreme Court, what was looking rather doubtful for the Democrats in November has given them something to be hopeful about...and <a href=\"http://www.politico.com/story/2014/06/scotus-decision-hobby-lobby-democrats-fundraise-108451.html?hp=l4\" target=\"_blank\">to drool over</a>...<i>Welcome to the bizarro world of modern fundraising, where even bad news can be good for the bottom line. A loss in a big policy fight means outrage, anger and lots of small-dollar campaign contributions from riled-up grass-roots supporters.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T19:38:31-07:00","id":185310,"title":"","content":"Thank you, Jim.<br /> <br /> Any thoughts on the 9-0 decisions this season? Namely Riley v California (cell phones), NLRB v Noel Canning (recess appointees), and McCullen v Coakley (buffer zones).<br /> <br /> What I find interesting is that those decisions, while unanimous, had different lines of reasoning to reach them for many members of the court (that is, some Justices dissented on how the rule should be applied, but came to the same conclusions in these cases).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T20:38:03-07:00","id":185322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185288#post185288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> RFRA.</div> </div> </div> </div>Care to cite any part of the RFRA that sets a precedent that for-profit non-religious closely held corporations have the right to exercise religious freedoms to the determent of their employees to exercise their religious freedoms?<br /> <br /> What is the legal justification for ruling that such corporations have this right?<br /> <br /> As well, the RFRA basically sets a strict scrutiny standard on this manners; not absolute protection. The government does have an overriding interest in ensuring access to birth control medication to women that want it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like? </div> </div> </div>I participated in starting an interfaith club in college... no corporation necessary for us to exercise our efforts to both promote tolerance and understanding of religious beliefs and petition the school's administration in circumstances where minority religions weren't being respected.<br /> <br /> Boom organized activism on religious expression without needing a for-profit corporation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of things could do that... a nuclear weapon, for instance. </div> </div> </div>We don't allow private individuals or corporations to own nuclear weapons despite the second amendment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What power? </div> </div> </div>The power do decide on their employee's healthcare options based on &quot;sincere&quot; beliefs. And while the Court's ruling is narrowly construed to contraception (for no possibly sustainable legal rationale, and clearly biased against women since it doesn't apply to things like condoms or spermicides which don't require prescription coverage to get), it still represents power they didn't have before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How do you know they didn't? Did you read the ruling? </div> </div> </div>Yes, and their response is basically that they know it could potentially harm millions of people and that it doesn't matter because they need to protect a much smaller group from the massive pain of something they're already doing indirectly anyway and could have compromised on with the existing exemptions in the ACA (which has a compromise option for religious objections that balances the corporation's owners' interests and the interests of the employees).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If that's so and they obeyed the law-as-written (which the majority will argue they did with RFRA and 1st Amendment), then wouldn't putting the interests of the employee above the minority, the employer, create law? </div> </div> </div>See above on the RFRA and scrutiny.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They are not only cited... </div> </div> </div>Experts are cited frequently in cases. That doesn't nullify my point. Madison wrote an expert opinion. His opinions have weight even today, but that's all they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They have? Like? And yes, the power to incorporate is explicitly granted by the Constitution... as a measure and power of the Federal Government that exists to allow the Government (a corporation, albeit a very special one) to exist as well. </div> </div> </div>Care to actually put a meaningful statement in there somewhere. This isn't debate club. Witty semantic jabs aren't going to score points.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Meanwhile, this can be nothing but a sincerely held belief or else it would make little to no sense to do, given that you're paying less than $50 an employee (see the video I linked earlier, maybe 2 hours in or so... I think). </div> </div> </div>How do you legally determine what is, and is not, a sincerely held belief?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Second, there is something stopping the government. The same ultimate check that exists to control the government on everything: the people. </div> </div> </div>We're a pretty weak check at the moment. Ideally you'd be right. In reality this is not sufficient.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yet this forum could probably use a lot of education (except maybe the Canuck) on legal theory before they nerd rage... especially if they want to change things. </div> </div> </div>So you want meaningful discussion from people you don't think are capable of it?<br /> <br /> Yeah... that's a big sign you are probably devil's advocating in the wrong place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T21:18:34-07:00","id":185338,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185322#post185322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Care to cite any part of the RFRA that sets a precedent that for-profit non-religious closely held corporations have the right to exercise religious freedoms to the determent of their employees to exercise their religious freedoms?</div> </div> </div> </div>2000bb-1 now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What is the legal justification for ruling that such corporations have this right? </div> </div> </div> That a closely held corporation exists as an extension of its owner's will and to deny its owner the ability to exercise his religion, without the state proving a compelling reason for which there is no easier burden to be placed, is to deny his ability to exercise his religion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As well, the RFRA basically sets a strict scrutiny standard on this manners; not absolute protection. The government does have an overriding interest in ensuring access to birth control medication to women that want it. </div> </div> </div> Yet it also has, within its means, a way of implementing that which would directly burden the owner less, failing the 2nd part of the RFRA test.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I participated in starting an interfaith club in college... no corporation necessary for us to exercise our efforts to both promote tolerance and understanding of religious beliefs and petition the school's administration in circumstances where minority religions weren't being respected. </div> </div> </div> Was your interfaith club chartered at all? Did it have a mission statement and was it a recognized club on the university grounds? If so, believe it or not, you were a recognized corporation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Boom organized activism on religious expression without needing a for-profit corporation. </div> </div> </div> Which can be done, but does that mean you cannot be a for-profit corporation practicing a religious expression? If not, why not?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We don't allow private individuals or corporations to own nuclear weapons despite the second amendment. </div> </div> </div> 42 USC 2131 provides the means for which private individuals and corporations can own nuclear weapons. I know that one. Does not mean you'll get one, but the mechanism is there to obtain it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The power do decide on their employee's healthcare options based on &quot;sincere&quot; beliefs. And while the Court's ruling is narrowly construed to contraception (for no possibly sustainable legal rationale, and clearly biased against women since it doesn't apply to things like condoms or spermicides which don't require prescription coverage to get), it still represents power they didn't have before. </div> </div> </div> Who says it doesn't apply to condoms or spermicides? If you wanted to use your health coverage to get them free, Hobby Lobby could deny that too. Just because one requires a prescription and the other does not does not mean the law isn't being applied equally. That's now how equal protection works. Equal protection functions where conditions are equal. Condoms and spermicides are not the same as a hormone treatment, which would also require a prescription in a male.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, and their response is basically that they know it could potentially harm millions of people and that it doesn't matter because they need to protect a much smaller group from the massive pain of something they're already doing indirectly anyway and could have compromised on with the existing exemptions in the ACA (which has a compromise option for religious objections that balances the corporation's owners' interests and the interests of the employees). </div> </div> </div> Citation within the document? Because that's not how I read it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> See above on the RFRA and scrutiny. </div> </div> </div> I fail to see the germaness.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Experts are cited frequently in cases. That doesn't nullify my point. Madison wrote an expert opinion. His opinions have weight even today, but that's all they are. </div> </div> </div> The court has used Madison's writings, alone, over 67 times, to create the opinion of the court. Madison's writings have been used to overturn a law over 35 times. They are permissible evidence when you are attempting to get to the heart of a constitutional matter.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Care to actually put a meaningful statement in there somewhere. This isn't debate club. Witty semantic jabs aren't going to score points. </div> </div> </div> My words had meaning. Did I fail to communicate it properly? If so, please let me know where and I'll be happy to elaborate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How do you legally determine what is, and is not, a sincerely held belief? </div> </div> </div> Watch the court hearing. They have a debate on just that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We're a pretty weak check at the moment. Ideally you'd be right. In reality this is not sufficient. </div> </div> </div> Are the people a weak check, or is your political ideology a weak check? I contend the people are working more or less as intended at the moment and that what you're seeing as a deep, and growing, ideological divide among the people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So you want meaningful discussion from people you don't think are capable of it?<br /> <br /> Yeah... that's a big sign you are probably devil's advocating in the wrong place. </div> </div> </div>You presume to know my motivations and make judgments based on that which are neither mine nor a product of mine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":".  wrong word. 07-01-2014, 12:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T21:26:06-07:00","id":185343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185308#post185308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And yet you defend such an opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div> I am? I thought I was calling into question the reactions, which is not equivalent to defending the opposing view, though it shares superficial similarities.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And claim that you're more educated on this than those that oppose it. (except &quot;maybe&quot; the Canuck) But it's all about how well it's presented. </div> </div> </div> I did? Where? Claiming a group needs more education does not make oneself above that group. One can recognize one's own need for education as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And if it looks and smells like poo for most women, then I should think that it is what it is. I don't need a taste test here. </div> </div> </div> That way leads to confirmation bias. Remember, you can be correct sometimes and still be biased.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Politics will be as usual..at play. And thanks to the Supreme Court, what was looking rather doubtful for the Democrats in November has given them something to be hopeful about...and <a href=\"http://www.politico.com/story/2014/06/scotus-decision-hobby-lobby-democrats-fundraise-108451.html?hp=l4\" target=\"_blank\">to drool over</a>...<i>Welcome to the bizarro world of modern fundraising, where even bad news can be good for the bottom line. A loss in a big policy fight means outrage, anger and lots of small-dollar campaign contributions from riled-up grass-roots supporters.</i> </div> </div> </div>We'll see, said the zen master. People on the old White Wolf forum said much the same in the 2010 election and... well... with the exception of the Senate and no endorsement of either party, I called that one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 07-01-2014, 12:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-30T23:03:09-07:00","id":185377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185338#post185338\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2000bb-1 now.</div> </div> </div> </div>And with that &quot;now&quot; I'll just take it that you're conceding the point that there's not legal justification for this in the existing laws before the ruling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That a closely held corporation exists as an extension of its owner's will and to deny its owner the ability to exercise his religion, without the state proving a compelling reason for which there is no easier burden to be placed, is to deny his ability to exercise his religion. </div> </div> </div>Why does closely held matter here?<br /> <br /> What is the legal justification for closely held corporations getting unique rights? Why don't fully publicly traded corporations get to vote on this at board meetings?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Was your interfaith club chartered at all? Did it have a mission statement and was it a recognized club on the university grounds? </div> </div> </div>No, yes, no.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which can be done, but does that mean you cannot be a for-profit corporation practicing a religious expression? If not, why not? </div> </div> </div>I've never argued this. I'm arguing that the government has the right the put the religious expressions of the employees over the expressions of the business owners.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 42 USC 2131 provides the means for which private individuals and corporations can own nuclear weapons. I know that one. Does not mean you'll get one, but the mechanism is there to obtain it. </div> </div> </div>This lovely tangent reinforces my point. You don't have the right to own such things. You can get a license to. You have to ask the government for permission, the government doesn't have to prove that you shouldn't be allowed to have one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Who says it doesn't apply to condoms or spermicides? </div> </div> </div>The ruling is fairly specific that it doesn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you wanted to use your health coverage to get them free, Hobby Lobby could deny that too. </div> </div> </div>Since they don't have any sincere beliefs about them being wrong; no they can't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just because one requires a prescription and the other does not does not mean the law isn't being applied equally. </div> </div> </div>The fact that it only applies to contraceptives that women can use however most certainly does. The ruling was specifically narrow on just women's health provisions in the ACA.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Citation within the document? Because that's not how I read it. </div> </div> </div>That's nice. But that's just interpretation. The majority opinion's dismissals of the dissenting opinions are insulting to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I fail to see the germaness </div> </div> </div>Of course not. I didn't make a point that lets you berate me on how much better you know legal stuff than me; since apparently that's what you want out of &quot;discussion.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The court has used Madison's writings, alone, over 67 times, to create the opinion of the court. Madison's writings have been used to overturn a law over 35 times. They are permissible evidence when you are attempting to get to the heart of a constitutional matter. </div> </div> </div>So... you agree with me? Awesome.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My words had meaning. </div> </div> </div>Oooo. Another failed pithy semantic jab. How nice.<br /> <br /> Your words had meaning. The phrases and sentences you constructed with them didn't have any relevant ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did I fail to communicate it properly? If so, please let me know where and I'll be happy to elaborate. </div> </div> </div>You have failed to communicate how the &quot;incorporation&quot; of the US government via the Constitution results in the constitutional basis for the existence of corporations. All you've managed to communicate is a willingness to use semantic equivocations to be dismissive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Watch the court hearing. They have a debate on just that. </div> </div> </div>Words were had on the subject. But I'll actually agree with the majority opinion here and their assessment that HHS did jack shit to make this argument (though I think the majority's dismissal of the amici briefs that actually bothered to do this sufficiently is a bit odd as they basically conceded that there was a good point for the dissent but since HHS didn't make it they could ignore it).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Are the people a weak check, or is your political ideology a weak check? I contend the people are working more or less as intended at the moment and that what you're seeing as a deep, and growing, ideological divide among the people. </div> </div> </div>I live in a state where there's roughly 50/50 divide along political lines voter wise. We actually have one Senator from each major political party. However the split in the House is ~28/72. And my state is far from unique in this. The majority of voters in the country voted for one party, but that party is not in control of the House of Representatives. That basically puts a big hole in the power of the people right there; since even with the people saying &quot;we want these guys, not those guys&quot; those guys are the ones in power.<br /> <br /> Then you have the use of the filibuster in the Senate were the minority routinely blocks actions, again, means the majority, aka the will of the people, is blocked.<br /> <br /> So no, this is not some flaw in my political ideology. The system is presently structured so a minority can block the people from being a check.<br /> <br /> And then you get into the problem of money in politics, where the people are routinely drowned out by wealthy interests.<br /> <br /> So yeah, we're a weak check when the majority opinion of the people is routinely ignored. This as nothing to do with my ideology.<br /> <br /> And really, the polarization factor isn't relevant to this. It doesn't matter how polarized the nation is. The majority isn't being listened to even on issues the country isn't polarized on. Things that poll with 90% approval don't get passed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You presume to know my motivations and make judgments based on that which are neither mine nor a product of mine. </div> </div> </div>You stated your motivations and my judgement are based on your words. Which are most certainly produced by you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T04:27:47-07:00","id":185443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185377#post185377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And with that &quot;now&quot; I'll just take it that you're conceding the point that there's not legal justification for this in the existing laws before the ruling.</div> </div> </div> </div> No, just taking the easiest way. There's history and precedence before that, and before RFRA even existed.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why does closely held matter here?<br /> <br /> What is the legal justification for closely held corporations getting unique rights? Why don't fully publicly traded corporations get to vote on this at board meetings? </div> </div> </div> Because that's the extent of the ruling and the court limited it to them for reasons listed in the court judgment... which I believe you did not read for reasons I'll get to in a moment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've never argued this. I'm arguing that the government has the right the put the religious expressions of the employees over the expressions of the business owners. </div> </div> </div> Why? What gives them the right to listen to one person over another? Were not tyranny of the majority and tyranny of the minority both issues the framers dealt with and we have continued to consider through the 14th amendment?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This lovely tangent reinforces my point. You don't have the right to own such things. You can get a license to. You have to ask the government for permission, the government doesn't have to prove that you shouldn't be allowed to have one. </div> </div> </div> Actually you do, and then you, as a corporate body through the government, cede that right to the government for a compelling interest that is not in violation of the Constitution and is not an inalienable right. And as someone who has written those justifications before, yes, the government does have to prove you shouldn't be allowed to have one (but the burden of proof in this extreme case, is very small).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The ruling is fairly specific that it doesn't. </div> </div> </div> Where?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since they don't have any sincere beliefs about them being wrong; no they can't. </div> </div> </div> Supposing they were based on the teaching of Catholicism, then in this legal scenario we've created... yes, they could.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fact that it only applies to contraceptives that women can use however most certainly does. The ruling was specifically narrow on just women's health provisions in the ACA. </div> </div> </div> So you'd like to it apply equally to two unequal things, one which is a hormonal treatment which should be monitored for very real health risks (ask my wife) and something that, by induction, most people never have a problem with beyond a rash (spermicide) or is functionally an article of clothing (condoms)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's nice. But that's just interpretation. The majority opinion's dismissals of the dissenting opinions are insulting to me. </div> </div> </div> Only you can allow yourself to be insulted.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course not. I didn't make a point that lets you berate me on how much better you know legal stuff than me; since apparently that's what you want out of &quot;discussion.&quot; </div> </div> </div> You have inferred that incorrectly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So... you agree with me? Awesome. </div> </div> </div>Yes in part, no in spirit. I agree they are not case law, because they were not passed in cases. However, you said, &quot;What Madison thought doesn't matter to what the law should be.&quot; You were wrong on the spirit of your expository statement, the heart of which was right there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Oooo. Another failed pithy semantic jab. How nice.<br /> <br /> Your words had meaning. The phrases and sentences you constructed with them didn't have any relevant ones. </div> </div> </div> Most certainly they did.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You have failed to communicate how the &quot;incorporation&quot; of the US government via the Constitution results in the constitutional basis for the existence of corporations. All you've managed to communicate is a willingness to use semantic equivocations to be dismissive. </div> </div> </div> The very existence of the government implies such authority exists, John Marshall himself wrote when reviewing charters issued by writ.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Words were had on the subject. But I'll actually agree with the majority opinion here and their assessment that HHS did jack shit to make this argument (though I think the majority's dismissal of the amici briefs that actually bothered to do this sufficiently is a bit odd as they basically conceded that there was a good point for the dissent but since HHS didn't make it they could ignore it). </div> </div> </div> Why didn't they? What was their argument, laid out logically, from within the hearing?<br /> <br /> So you're angry that dismissed some amici briefs and went with others, like nearly every court ever?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I live in a state where there's roughly 50/50 divide along political lines voter wise. We actually have one Senator from each major political party. However the split in the House is ~28/72. And my state is far from unique in this. The majority of voters in the country voted for one party, but that party is not in control of the House of Representatives. That basically puts a big hole in the power of the people right there; since even with the people saying &quot;we want these guys, not those guys&quot; those guys are the ones in power. </div> </div> </div> What you basically said here is that the system is working as intended by the framers of the constitution. The House could be checked for popular opinion every 2 years, 1/3 of the Senate every 2 years, and the President every 4 years. Elections are not an end-all-be-all (usually). They are a snapshot in time of a moving electorate's opinions.<br /> <br /> In 2012, the majority of voters clearly did not favor one party or another for the Congress, as the Congress did not have a major adjustment. In 2010, the majority favored in the Republicans. In 2008, the majority favored the Democrats in both Presidential and Congressional.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then you have the use of the filibuster in the Senate were the minority routinely blocks actions, again, means the majority, aka the will of the people, is blocked. </div> </div> </div> That's a nice topic shift, but we can go there if you want. I think I can even dig up the old White Wolf forum debates on it and probably just copy and paste from those for the next 10 pages.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So no, this is not some flaw in my political ideology. The system is presently structured so a minority can block the people from being a check. </div> </div> </div> The minority cannot block the people. They can block the majority party. These two statements are unequal. Why? Because if you block the majority enough times and the people disagree with you, they will vote you out. Perhaps you are simply angry that the people do not agree with you in a large enough number?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And then you get into the problem of money in politics, where the people are routinely drowned out by wealthy interests. </div> </div> </div> A known problem since the founding of the Republic and one which the Founding Fathers considered to be troubling, but ultimately a better problem to have than the opposite problems which can arise (again, Federalist 10 and other writings of Jefferson, and a few others).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So yeah, we're a weak check when the majority opinion of the people is routinely ignored. This as nothing to do with my ideology. </div> </div> </div> Except that you assume your ideology is the majority opinion of the actual people voting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And really, the polarization factor isn't relevant to this. It doesn't matter how polarized the nation is. The majority isn't being listened to even on issues the country isn't polarized on. Things that poll with 90% approval don't get passed. </div> </div> </div> Only 1 poll actually matters, and it certainly didn't get a 90% approval rating for either party.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You stated your motivations and my judgement are based on your words. Which are most certainly produced by you. </div> </div> </div> Again, what you infer from my words is not equal to what I am implying with them. What you're reading beyond the words on the screen, namely deeper motivation than, &quot;Pretty much everyone should learn more about how the law works,&quot; is your villanization of me, based less on reality and more on what you seem to want to be true.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T05:07:48-07:00","id":185449,"title":"","content":"No matter how narrowly they define this specific case, it will be used as precedence for arguing exclusion from providing other employee rights based on religion.<br /> <br /> This all originates from giving individual rights to business entities. No matter the justification, that will ever be wrong. <a href=\"http://www.huffingtonpost.com/adam-winkler/corporations-are-people-a_b_5543833.html\" target=\"_blank\">Corporations Are People, And They Have More Rights Than You</a><br /> <i><br /> Ever since Citizens United, the Supreme Court's 2010 decision allowing unlimited corporate and union spending on political issues, Americans have been debating whether, as Mitt Romney said, &quot;Corporations are people, my friend.&quot; Occupy Wall Street protestors decried the idea, late night comedians mocked it, and reform groups proposed amending the Constitution to eliminate it. Today, however, the Supreme Court endorsed corporate personhood -- holding that business firms have rights to religious freedom under federal law. Not only do corporations have rights, their rights are stronger than yours.<br /> And women may not be the only victims. What religious rights will business corporations seek next? The Court said that its decision wouldn't necessarily mean that closely-held businesses could obtain exemptions from health care regulations mandating insurance coverage for vaccinations and blood transfusions. Yet the Court did grant those corporations today a right to make such claims in court. If ensuring women's control over reproduction -- a constitutional right -- isn't a strong enough reason to limit the religious rights of Hobby Lobby, it's not clear why these other laws won't fall too.<br /> LGBT people may be next. Remember a few months ago when Arizona almost adopted a controversial law that would have given business corporations a broad right to use religion to make claims for exemptions from the law? That proposed law was rightly seen as an attack on LGBT rights, as supporters insisted that business owners who object to same-sex marriage shouldn't be forced to bake cakes, take pictures, or arrange the flowers at such ceremonies. After Hobby Lobby, now all business corporations have a right under federal law to claim religious-based exemptions to all sorts of laws -- including laws prohibiting discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation.</i><br /> <br /> --------------------<br /> <br /> <i>Ever since the democratic systems permitted their various courts to give corporations the status of persons, the individual as citizen has been on the defensive. How could it be otherwise? If you are a person before the law and Exxon or Ford is also a person, it is clear that the concept of democratic legitimacy lying with the individual has been mortally wounded. <br /> --John Ralston Saul </i><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T06:31:43-07:00","id":185482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184748#post184748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although it's good to hear the wealthy are finally getting nervous; <a href=\"http://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/06/the-pitchforks-are-coming-for-us-plutocrats-108014.html\" target=\"_blank\">The Pitchforks are Coming for Us Plutocrats</a></div> </div> </div> </div>How I wish we had learned any lessons from the 20th century. I just hope next time we try Marxian theory in practice, we display the prerequisite democratic reflexes not to have a second wave of Stalinism.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T07:13:26-07:00","id":185495,"title":"","content":"I have just one thought on this decision: How long before Wal-Mart decides to play the same hand?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T07:25:23-07:00","id":185503,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185449#post185449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No matter how narrowly they define this specific case, it will be used as precedence for arguing exclusion from providing other employee rights based on religion.<br /> <br /> This all originates from giving individual rights to business entities. No matter the justification, that will ever be wrong. <a href=\"http://www.huffingtonpost.com/adam-winkler/corporations-are-people-a_b_5543833.html\" target=\"_blank\">Corporations Are People, And They Have More Rights Than You</a></div> </div> </div> </div> So... apparently the article doesn't know the origin of corporate personhood? It wasn't Citizens United. Hell, it wasn't even in the 20th century.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>Ever since the democratic systems permitted their various courts to give corporations the status of persons, the individual as citizen has been on the defensive. How could it be otherwise? If you are a person before the law and Exxon or Ford is also a person, it is clear that the concept of democratic legitimacy lying with the individual has been mortally wounded.<br /> --John Ralston Saul </i> </div> </div> </div>Ahh, Saul. Good man. Heard him speak in my neck of the woods once. Interesting ideas, but he's had a bit of confusion on what corporate personhood actually means... though he does make some interesting cases in his essays. They're worth reading.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":".  Missing quote bracket. 07-01-2014, 10:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T07:27:11-07:00","id":185504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have just one thought on this decision: How long before Wal-Mart decides to play the same hand? </div> </div> </div> WalMart is not a closely held corporation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T07:40:15-07:00","id":185508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185504#post185504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> WalMart is not a closely held corporation.</div> </div> </div> </div>As the above posts have demonstrated, &quot;closely held&quot; can be interpreted in a few different ways. Are you 100% positive that less than 50% of the Wal-Mart stocks are held by one or more of Sam Walton's kids?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T07:45:24-07:00","id":185510,"title":"","content":"No. However, that is not the only qualification, despite what Huff Post and anyone else who just quotes the IRS FAQ would have you believe. Check the pub I listed (I'll wait a few months, it's long).<br /> <br /> Additionally, Walmart was one of the largest supports of the ACA. Why would they try to opt out when it demonstrably lowered their costs and increased their competitiveness? ACA benefited larger corporations far more than smaller ones, to date.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T09:47:04-07:00","id":185607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185443#post185443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> No, just taking the easiest way.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, you're taking the &quot;way&quot; of not engaging in the discussion you say you want by throwing out shit arguments instead of seriously discussing anything.<br /> <br /> Hell you're willing to argue both for and against the &quot;extent of the ruling and the court limited it&quot; when it suits you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why? What gives them the right to listen to one person over another? </div> </div> </div>That's their job as the Court. To pick between two sides. If the case shouldn't come before them because they shouldn't be making those decisions for a particular case, they shouldn't take up the case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where? </div> </div> </div>Says the one accusing me of not reading the ruling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Only you can allow yourself to be insulted. </div> </div> </div>Bullshit. Though it is fairly in keeping with:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You have inferred that incorrectly. </div> </div> </div>Then phrase it better, because at the moment your phrasing does not communicate what you're saying was your intended meaning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes in part, no in spirit. </div> </div> </div>So we have different views on constitutional interpretation; that doesn't make me wrong or you right (or the other way around as none of the schools on this have an authoritative claim). To me, while understanding the framers is important, combing through their essays in exercises of confirmation bias is folly. The world we live in today if vastly different from theirs and what they thought about anything 200+ years ago cannot be directly transferred as what they would think about the situation now. Madison is important for understanding why the law was written the way it was. Anything he said about corporations isn't terribly important because what a corporation was when he wrote his essays was vastly different.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The very existence of the government implies such authority exists, John Marshall himself wrote when reviewing charters issued by writ. </div> </div> </div>The authority to create corporations exists. Which isn't the same as corporations having defined status and rights simply because the government exists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So you're angry that dismissed some amici briefs and went with others, like nearly every court ever? </div> </div> </div>I think it is poor judicial reasoning to throw out amici briefs that make important and relevant points so that you don't have to contend with those points even though they've been raised.<br /> <br /> I have no issue with the the Courts ability to toss said briefs in general, I have a problem with the specifics in this ruling (though not limited to this ruling).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What you basically said here is that the system is working as intended by the framers of the constitution. </div> </div> </div>Where, exactly, did the framers state the intention that the House of Representatives should not reflect the votes cast? Do you really think the framers intended that should a state go 50/50 in their votes for the House that the end results would not be reasonably close to that ratio of Congressmen? I'm curious what the basis of this belief is.<br /> <br /> The frequency of &quot;snapshots&quot; is irrelevant if the snapshots aren't accurate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In 2012, the majority of voters clearly did not favor one party or another for the Congress </div> </div> </div>Nationally, the Democrats had a 1.2% lead in votes, which is a slim, but clear number. They ended up with 33 less seats than the Republicans, while the voting &quot;snap shot&quot; said they should have a 5-6 seat majority instead. Even without either side managing a majority of the vote, the votes cast are not reflected in the results of how each party is represented.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's a nice topic shift, but we can go there if you want. </div> </div> </div>It's not a topic shift. It's a point of evidence in the case that our current system makes the people a weak check on the government.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because if you block the majority enough times and the people disagree with you, they will vote you out. </div> </div> </div>I guess you really haven't be paying attention to issues like gerrymandering...<br /> <br /> Again, this is a structural issue. It doesn't matter if the majority of the people disagree with you if you're sitting in a nice solidly gerrymandered district where you're only beholden to the minority view point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps you are simply angry that the people do not agree with you in a large enough number? </div> </div> </div>Perhaps you should stop inferring people's emotions if you want to complain about us inferring your motivations incorrectly.<br /> <br /> And, again, trying to turn this into an argument about &quot;me&quot; is crap. If you can't argue the point on the actual facts, what the hell do you care about legal discussions?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A known problem since the founding of the Republic and one which the Founding Fathers considered to be troubling, but ultimately a better problem to have than the opposite problems which can arise (again, Federalist 10 and other writings of Jefferson, and a few others). </div> </div> </div>That doesn't justify making and/or letting the problem get worse when there's no reason to do so. Nor does it disprove the idea that the power of the people as a check has been sufficiently undermined as to be considered weak.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except that you assume your ideology is the majority opinion of the actual people voting. </div> </div> </div>No, I don't. How the hell is this even part of my unstated ideology anyway? If you want to make a point about me making assumptions about voting, do that. Stop with this ridiculous &quot;ideology&quot; ad hominem crap.<br /> <br /> I don't need to assume anything. I haven't said what my political ideology even is. I know plenty of people of opposing ideologies that still agree that the level of gerrymandering in my state is contrary to representative democracy and harms the political process by pushing politicians to cater to minority positions to maintain their seats in tightly held districts rather than having to worry about what the majority of their own party's members actually think (because this effects both major political parties). And there's plenty of numbers to back this up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, what you infer from my words is not equal to what I am implying with them. </div> </div> </div>If you want to be clear, it is your burden to ensure that you state things clearly rather than implying them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What you're reading beyond the words on the screen, namely deeper motivation than, &quot;Pretty much everyone should learn more about how the law works,&quot; is your villanization of me, based less on reality and more on what you seem to want to be true. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps you should avoid terms like, &quot;nerd rage&quot; then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T09:59:13-07:00","id":185611,"title":"","content":"Citizen's United has pretty much given us another <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lochner_era\" target=\"_blank\">Lochner Era</a>. Unfortunately I think it'll take more than the 3 decades to undo. Too many idiots in congress (as well as the current president) still believe in that crazy deranged &quot;free trade&quot; crap.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T16:01:25-07:00","id":185827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185607#post185607\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, you're taking the &quot;way&quot; of not engaging in the discussion you say you want by throwing out shit arguments instead of seriously discussing anything.<br /> <br /> Hell you're willing to argue both for and against the &quot;extent of the ruling and the court limited it&quot; when it suits you.</div> </div> </div> </div> Oh? You do want a serious discussion?<br /> <br /> And I would say I'm not arguing out of both sides of my mouth so much as trying to explain where the limits and extensions of the law exist in the context of this law.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's their job as the Court. To pick between two sides. If the case shouldn't come before them because they shouldn't be making those decisions for a particular case, they shouldn't take up the case. </div> </div> </div> So you believe the job of the court is to pick between two sides and to choose the majority? And are you implying that this case had no standing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Says the one accusing me of not reading the ruling. </div> </div> </div> Which is precisely my point: where? The ruling doesn't mention spermicides or condoms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then phrase it better, because at the moment your phrasing does not communicate what you're saying was your intended meaning. </div> </div> </div> Then ask me for clarification instead of assuming you know what I mean.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So we have different views on constitutional interpretation; that doesn't make me wrong or you right (or the other way around as none of the schools on this have an authoritative claim). To me, while understanding the framers is important, combing through their essays in exercises of confirmation bias is folly. The world we live in today if vastly different from theirs and what they thought about anything 200+ years ago cannot be directly transferred as what they would think about the situation now. Madison is important for understanding why the law was written the way it was. Anything he said about corporations isn't terribly important because what a corporation was when he wrote his essays was vastly different. </div> </div> </div>You said, &quot;What Madison thought doesn't matter to what the law should be.&quot; You were, prima facie, wrong on that point. If you meant it shouldn't matter, then we can talk about views on constitutional interpretation. I haven't stated a view on whether the Federalist papers should or should not matter, I have stated a fact that they do and have in over 206 court cases.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The authority to create corporations exists. Which isn't the same as corporations having defined status and rights simply because the government exists. </div> </div> </div> Except that's where the Court created that legal fiction from in 1819.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think it is poor judicial reasoning to throw out amici briefs that make important and relevant points so that you don't have to contend with those points even though they've been raised.<br /> <br /> I have no issue with the the Courts ability to toss said briefs in general, I have a problem with the specifics in this ruling (though not limited to this ruling). </div> </div> </div> So you have a problem with just about every ruling ever, or have I misunderstood? Because the logic you have used here applies to well over 90% of all rulings the high court has ever made (excluding summary judgments).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where, exactly, did the framers state the intention that the House of Representatives should not reflect the votes cast? Do you really think the framers intended that should a state go 50/50 in their votes for the House that the end results would not be reasonably close to that ratio of Congressmen? I'm curious what the basis of this belief is. </div> </div> </div> Yes. The House was intended to be a local representative sent to Congress. It doesn't even matter what the state wants, it matters what the local district wants. So, yes, you can very well have a situation where 50% of the state voted for one party and the votes don't reflect that in the House, depending on where the population of the state is concentrated. Now if you want to talk about gerrymandering and the size of districts that is, again, a different topic with a different history, but we can.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The frequency of &quot;snapshots&quot; is irrelevant if the snapshots aren't accurate. </div> </div> </div> Except it's the only accurate snapshot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nationally, the Democrats had a 1.2% lead in votes, which is a slim, but clear number. </div> </div> </div> And it doesn't matter. The will of the people is expressed in state, local, and Congressional elections as befits and regards that state, not the nation. The nation only votes, as a whole, on the President, and even that was designed to prevent a tyranny of the majority from the large states towards the small. Whether or not that system (electoral college) is working anymore is a different, but related topic. The House and the Senate are not affected by the electoral college.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They ended up with 33 less seats than the Republicans, while the voting &quot;snap shot&quot; said they should have a 5-6 seat majority instead. Even without either side managing a majority of the vote, the votes cast are not reflected in the results of how each party is represented. </div> </div> </div> ...I'm sensing you don't understand how elections work. Perhaps I am wrong, but you seem to be conflating several numbers that don't actually matter to how our system of elections works and how the will of the people was purposefully designed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not a topic shift. It's a point of evidence in the case that our current system makes the people a weak check on the government. </div> </div> </div> The filibuster is a topic shift as it has very little to do with the people being a check or balance.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I guess you really haven't be paying attention to issues like gerrymandering... </div> </div> </div> Oh, I have. The historical significance of gerrymandering is also something that's quite interesting and worth studying, but hardly relevant here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, this is a structural issue. It doesn't matter if the majority of the people disagree with you if you're sitting in a nice solidly gerrymandered district where you're only beholden to the minority view point. </div> </div> </div> So in which Congressional election did the majority cast a vote against a Senator or Representative and he won election in 2006, 2008, 2010, or 2012?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps you should stop inferring people's emotions if you want to complain about us inferring your motivations incorrectly. </div> </div> </div> That's why mine was in the form of a question. It was intended to probe, not to say that is what's going on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, again, trying to turn this into an argument about &quot;me&quot; is crap. If you can't argue the point on the actual facts, what the hell do you care about legal discussions? </div> </div> </div> Seeing as how I have yet to run across compelling facts, I'm searching for deeper meaning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That doesn't justify making and/or letting the problem get worse when there's no reason to do so. Nor does it disprove the idea that the power of the people as a check has been sufficiently undermined as to be considered weak. </div> </div> </div> Actually, there's a rather detailed argument, by the founding fathers, that it does justify letting the problem get worse. To a point. Where is that point? I don't know. Have we crossed it? I don't know... but I'd bet if we have, it probably wasn't here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, I don't. How the hell is this even part of my unstated ideology anyway? If you want to make a point about me making assumptions about voting, do that. Stop with this ridiculous &quot;ideology&quot; ad hominem crap. </div> </div> </div> You're right, that was an assumption on my part to ask a question, and then follow through on that assumption. If my assumption was invalid, I'll drop the line of reasoning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't need to assume anything. I haven't said what my political ideology even is. I know plenty of people of opposing ideologies that still agree that the level of gerrymandering in my state is contrary to representative democracy and harms the political process by pushing politicians to cater to minority positions to maintain their seats in tightly held districts rather than having to worry about what the majority of their own party's members actually think (because this effects both major political parties). And there's plenty of numbers to back this up. </div> </div> </div> Like?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you want to be clear, it is your burden to ensure that you state things clearly rather than implying them. </div> </div> </div> I cannot change what you infer that is separate from what I wrote. If you read something that was simply not in the text I wrote, I cannot be held responsible for your conclusion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps you should avoid terms like, &quot;nerd rage&quot; then. </div> </div> </div> Why?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T16:03:39-07:00","id":185829,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185611#post185611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Citizen's United has pretty much given us another <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lochner_era\" target=\"_blank\">Lochner Era</a>. Unfortunately I think it'll take more than the 3 decades to undo. Too many idiots in congress (as well as the current president) still believe in that crazy deranged &quot;free trade&quot; crap.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually you can probably trace that back to the Civil Rights Act's fundamental shift in legislative and court powers. (Read absolutely nothing into that on the validity or merits of the Civil Rights Act. This statement is about its 2nd and 3rd tier effects, not its primary purpose.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T22:36:49-07:00","id":186049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=185827#post185827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Oh? You do want a serious discussion?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Not someone failing to be Socrates. You yourself acknowledged at the beginning of all this that you were possibly upsetting people. Yet you continued to post in a style you knew might derail discussion rather than modify. That colors how people interpret you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I would say I'm not arguing out of both sides of my mouth so much as trying to explain where the limits and extensions of the law exist in the context of this law. </div> </div> </div>Of course you would. I think you're failing utterly at it. Because you're not explaining anything in a meaningful fashion and holding others to a double standard.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I cannot change what you infer that is separate from what I wrote. If you read something that was simply not in the text I wrote, I cannot be held responsible for your conclusion.<br /> <br /> Why? </div> </div> </div>Because throwing out derogatory terms colors how people infer your meaning. It tells me you think people upset with this ruling are just &quot;nerd raging&quot; and don't care if they have valid issue with it or not. If you what me to think you want a serious discussion, consider that your entire approach to this has not been to actually discuss anything. All you've done is search for any hole you can in a person's posts to declare them legally ignorant. It doesn't matter if there's a technical wrong somewhere. This isn't law class. No one is getting graded. You don't &quot;win&quot; anything or even accomplish anything (in terms of your stated goal of expanding people's understanding of the law) by nitpicking in the fashion you're doing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T01:28:53-07:00","id":186120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=184888#post184888\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-----------------------<br /> I was more concerned over the Union ruling which still seems iffy; <a href=\"http://thehill.com/regulation/court-battles/210944-unions-dodge-bullet-at-supreme-court\" target=\"_blank\">Unions dodge bullet at Supreme Court</a><br /> <i><br /> In a 5-4 decision, the justices voided a requirement that personal medical assistants in Illinois contribute fees to a branch of Service Employees International Union (SEIU), even if they choose not to become members.<br /> But in a victory for labor, the justices did not directly address the broader question of whether public sector employees can be forced to pay union dues, leaving in place state laws aimed at preventing \u201cfree riding\u201d by non-members.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Kind of climbing your ass first up to tree approach those forced payments, I think... In Europe only union members pay union fees from their salary and people join them because they're smart but then again USA has never had proper worker unions just some neutered coffee clubs and mob fronts for what I can see.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1700-blue-fox","name":"Blue Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T06:24:20-07:00","id":186240,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=186049#post186049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes. Not someone failing to be Socrates.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does appear to be sophistry as it's seen today.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blue Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=186120#post186120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kind of climbing your ass first up to tree approach those forced payments, I think... In Europe only union members pay union fees from their salary and people join them because they're smart but then again USA has never had proper worker unions just some neutered coffee clubs and mob fronts for what I can see.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yea..over here big business has a level of power that is simply amazing. And unions do whatever they have to, to remain a political force. Unions were once the backbone for the middle class here. But because of politics, unions are doing a fast fade, and as a result the middle class is also fading away.<br /> <br /> It's why I see pieces of a puzzle in an above post. And it's not looking good. It took over 30 years to finally rid ourselves of the Lochner Era, and a pissed off president (Franklin Roosevelt that had a lot of political clout in congress) And this was a timeframe that had many in the US seeing &quot;socialism&quot; as a very interesting idea.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T09:43:35-07:00","id":186352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/183891-supreme-court-term-review-merged?p=186049#post186049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes. Not someone failing to be Socrates. You yourself acknowledged at the beginning of all this that you were possibly upsetting people. Yet you continued to post in a style you knew might derail discussion rather than modify. That colors how people interpret you.</div> </div> </div> </div> Upsetting them because I would be assuming a position opposite of their assumptions, not in the style of argumentation. Also, I never claimed to be Socrates.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course you would. I think you're failing utterly at it. Because you're not explaining anything in a meaningful fashion and holding others to a double standard. </div> </div> </div> I am? Where? (EDIT: Btw... I believe this would be an ad hominem... you know, that thing you (perhaps rightly) called me out on?)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because throwing out derogatory terms colors how people infer your meaning. It tells me you think people upset with this ruling are just &quot;nerd raging&quot; and don't care if they have valid issue with it or not. If you what me to think you want a serious discussion, consider that your entire approach to this has not been to actually discuss anything. All you've done is search for any hole you can in a person's posts to declare them legally ignorant. It doesn't matter if there's a technical wrong somewhere. This isn't law class. No one is getting graded. You don't &quot;win&quot; anything or even accomplish anything (in terms of your stated goal of expanding people's understanding of the law) by nitpicking in the fashion you're doing. </div> </div> </div> I didn't know nerd rage was a derogatory term so much as a term of endearment. Also, my words did not tell you I think people upset with this ruling are nerd raging. You inferred that. My words were that people understand what they're mad about and not simply &quot;nerd rage&quot; because it's the thing to do.<br /> <br /> I never said this was a law class or that anyone was being graded, merely that I see a lot of misunderstanding. Furiously picking at nits is often required in legal situations to gain a full and complete understanding.<br /> <br /> It's not sophistry if a bit of detail changes the context.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I feel like, somewhere along the way, <a href=\"http://dilbert.com/strips/comic/2011-07-02/\" target=\"_blank\">this became the conversation we're having...</a> (I'm Dilbert, from my point of view).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2058,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","user":"Nobody","date":". 07-02-2014, 07:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar